WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.540
Привет, Фрэннс, welcome to привет, русский, эпизод 42-17-ю не 2026 года.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.620
Я, Моля, это мы в Готсам Грэй Трашенворцы, чтобы

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:10.080
today.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:10.980
Поехали.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:11.580
Let's go.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:16.700
Our word of the day is солнце, солнце, it sounds like

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:22.580
солн-хол-нце, imagine the sun smiling down like a big friendly face, warming your cheeks.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:28.480
That is our numerы-хук, солнце светит ярко, the sun is shining brightly, repeat

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.620
after me, солнце, пауз, солнце светит ярко.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.720
Now let's build more weather words so you can talk about any day outside.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:43.260
Our next word is дождь, дождь, it sounds like дождь, дождь.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.280
It reminds us of drizzle on rainy days, дождь идет, it is raining.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:56.820
Next comes sneak, sneak, it sounds like snack, think of soft snow sneaking quietly down from

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:03.780
the sky, снег белый, the snow is white, here is ветер, ветер, it sounds like

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:10.100
вы-етор, а квик-брис раше спаст, just like the sound, ветер

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:17.500
do it, the wind is blowing, now we meet облака, облака, it sounds like

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:23.520
облака, clouds blanket the sky like a soft cover, and that helps us remember

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:31.960
it, облако, большой, the cloud is big, when the air feels warm we say жарко, жарко,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:39.640
it sounds like жарко, the heat can feel jarring on a summer day, сегодня жарко, it is hot

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:40.020
today.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:47.240
For the upset feeling we use холодно, холодно, it sounds like how lost now, it shares

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:53.240
the chilly start with the English word cold, зимой холодно, it is cold in winter.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:59.740
Было капанси неба, неба, it sounds like небиба, пикчера

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:05.980
enable of floating high above us, неба синие, the sky is blue, finally we learn the

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:11.220
big word that ties everything together, погода, погода, it sounds like

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:16.120
погода, good weather simply makes us feel good, погода хороше, the water is good,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:22.140
Russian often drops the verb is, when we describe the weather, we just say the action

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:28.340
straightaway, солнце светит, means the sun shines, дождь идёт, means rain goes,

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:35.400
or it rains, ветер-дует, means wind blows, this short sentences feel natural

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.880
and punchy once you try them, Russian uses action verbs like

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:45.560
идёт, and do it directly for the events, instead of always inserting the form of to be.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.820
This gives descriptions brief while remain clear to native speakers, the pattern works

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.320
for many simple statements about nature and daily conditions.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.440
In Russia, people love chatting about the weather because winter stay long and snowy,

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.300
while summers can turn surprising in a hot.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.940
They even say, погода шепчет, when the day feels just right for a walk outside.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:15.040
This phrase shows how weather forms a regular part of friendly conversation across the country.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:21.060
Russians notice seasonal shift closely because long winter shape daily routines and clothing choices.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.960
Summer brings their own strong heat that surprises visitors from cooler climates.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:31.780
Talking about these changes helps people connect over shared experiences of nature.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:33.500
Let's practice now.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.260
Draw a big sun, a cloud, and a snowflake.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.600
Then label each one with its Russian word and say it out loud three times.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:49.540
Or look out your window right now, and make one sentence about the days погода using two new words you just learned.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.960
Kids can turn the drawing into a quick game by pointing at each picture and repeating the labels.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:00.400
Adults contest themselves by describing current conditions without looking back at notes.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.880
Both approaches help lock the vocabulary into memory through immediate use.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.820
Now here is something really cool about one of today's words.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.460
Want to know secrets about неба?

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:18.800
Did you know that the Russian word неба, and the English word неба, are actually cousins from the same ancient route?

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.020
They both began as a word for cloud or mist.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.760
The idea travels through Latin and Greek into English science words.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.360
Yet stayed simple in Russian for everyday sky talk.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.280
Just remember not to mix it with heaven, which is rye in Russian.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:40.660
Next time you look up, picture the cloud in mist and you remember неба forever.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:48.240
The shared history gives learners an easy link between a common Russian term and a familiar English-scientific word.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.880
The ancient route described the misty dome overhead before branching into different modern languages.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:02.160
Keeping the everyday menu in Russian makes неба straightforward to use, while it's relatively low.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:05.080
Appears mainly in astronomy context.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.100
And that's our lesson for today.

